1
00:05:16,340 --> 00:05:47,300
Thank you very much, Bill Dill. Ladies and gentlemen, let me first say once more how grateful I am and delighted to be allowed back to what, after all these years, is still very much my home, and how very much I appreciate that I am still being permitted to come here every year and to be a member of the faculty and of the school for a few short days.

2
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:55,950
And usually, and I think I credit this to the GBA management, the weather is perfect.

3
00:05:56,990 --> 00:06:03,300
And let me say a welcome to all the many old friends I see in the audience.

4
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:08,760
I hope I'll get around to saying a few words to each of you in the course of the day.

5
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:20,980
But, quesamissio, let me say how happy I am to see so many people who have been friends of this school and of this lecture series and of myself for so many years.

6
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:37,580
And I want to say in particular how grateful I am to Earth Crystal to take time out from what must easily be the busiest schedule in the nation next to Mr. Carter's.

7
00:06:37,580 --> 00:06:39,200
to be here

8
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:41,020
and unlike Mr. Carter

9
00:06:41,020 --> 00:06:43,580
despite the hostages coming out

10
00:06:43,580 --> 00:06:45,960
and to be here

11
00:06:45,960 --> 00:06:47,580
with us because really

12
00:06:47,580 --> 00:06:49,680
this is his topic far more

13
00:06:49,680 --> 00:06:51,600
than mine and most

14
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:53,600
of what I know about the topic

15
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:55,560
I've really learned from reading

16
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:57,900
Professor Crystal over the years

17
00:06:57,900 --> 00:06:59,260
and he has

18
00:06:59,260 --> 00:07:01,580
enlightened, really

19
00:07:01,580 --> 00:07:03,660
given all of us a new perspective

20
00:07:03,660 --> 00:07:05,620
on the political process

21
00:07:05,620 --> 00:07:07,360
and on the changes

22
00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:09,360
in the political environment.

23
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:20,520
One can look at this topic of ours in two ways,

24
00:07:20,520 --> 00:07:27,920
and one would really be in terms of a political philosopher

25
00:07:27,920 --> 00:07:36,790
asking the question what has become of the modern national state

26
00:07:36,790 --> 00:07:39,770
of that proudest child of the modern age,

27
00:07:39,770 --> 00:07:48,270
which is clearly in an advanced stage of senility, premature senility, irreversible, I'm afraid.

28
00:07:48,890 --> 00:08:00,480
Or one can look and see, since one has to operate in what is and not in what was or in what should be

29
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:05,560
or in what will be, what does it look like today?

30
00:08:05,560 --> 00:08:26,880
And I thought the intelligent topic for this session is not the philosophical one in which one tries to discern why modern government has become both obese and incompetent,

31
00:08:26,880 --> 00:08:47,320
But in which one looks at the situation and says, if you have to run an institution, a business today, what are the rules that no longer apply? What are the rules that sometimes apply? And what are the new rules that again only sometimes apply?

32
00:08:47,320 --> 00:08:59,800
because I think one of the characteristics of this period is that there are no rules that one can use with even high probability.

33
00:09:01,140 --> 00:09:11,500
And if you look at the situation and the way of crystal is going to, I think, stress other aspects of the same situation,

34
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:19,900
And two things, or three, two certainly, strike me as being of particular importance

35
00:09:19,900 --> 00:09:25,820
and usually not seen by the executive in business.

36
00:09:25,820 --> 00:09:32,020
Let me say the greatest danger in turbulent times like ours is not the turbulence.

37
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:40,860
The greatest danger is that you act rationally in terms of yesterday.

38
00:09:40,860 --> 00:09:48,060
and that you assume that the things that have worked for so long

39
00:09:48,060 --> 00:09:54,060
and we have been through the longest period of continuity in economic history

40
00:09:54,060 --> 00:10:00,940
ever recorded from 1950 or 48 until the early 70s

41
00:10:00,940 --> 00:10:06,160
and then one comes to believe that the things that worked in that period

42
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:14,280
were really laid down on Mount Sinai, if not in the first six days of creation,

43
00:10:14,960 --> 00:10:24,200
and our laws of the Lord and laws of nature, whereas they were very temporary and transitory phenomena.

44
00:10:24,800 --> 00:10:34,500
And then one doesn't look, one reacts to a reality that no longer really prevails.

45
00:10:34,500 --> 00:10:57,980
And so I want to point out two things which most of my friends in businesses, especially top management in large businesses, who are very insulated, as I think you all know, take for granted and which simply are at least not solid anymore.

46
00:10:57,980 --> 00:11:14,260
The first is a structure of the population in which there was a constituency for business,

47
00:11:15,780 --> 00:11:23,660
a business constituency that in a way began with the owner of the corner cigar store

48
00:11:23,660 --> 00:11:29,560
and consisted of individuals who had a stake in business.

49
00:11:29,560 --> 00:11:47,990
And when you read the rhetoric of business leadership, including the Business Mount Table, for which I have great respect, and when you look at the lobbyists and so on, they all still assume this.

50
00:11:47,990 --> 00:12:10,880
And then one has to say very bluntly that there is no such constituency and that, in fact, no group in American society is as alienated, to use a nice, fashionable term, from big business.

51
00:12:10,880 --> 00:12:16,380
And what big business is, is a very debatable question.

52
00:12:16,780 --> 00:12:18,520
Believe me, it isn't just GM.

53
00:12:19,080 --> 00:12:25,200
The companies which you consider small business are seen as big business.

54
00:12:26,220 --> 00:12:31,480
Anything over a few hundred employees or 500 employees or so,

55
00:12:31,660 --> 00:12:41,230
where it just sees itself in terms of management and marketing and finance is big business.

56
00:12:41,230 --> 00:13:05,050
And the cigar store owner or his replacement, the fellow who owns five supermarkets in Portland, Oregon, does not see any identity either of point of view or of concerns or of perspective with them.

57
00:13:05,050 --> 00:13:24,440
There's a very sharp break and it is simply a result in large fact, a large measure of the fact that ownership of big business is not in the hands of individuals but in the hands of intermediaries

58
00:13:24,440 --> 00:13:39,750
who look quite different from that ownership of the very large,

59
00:13:39,810 --> 00:13:41,650
in number, small business sector,

60
00:13:42,090 --> 00:13:47,750
which large business sees as its normal conservative constituency.

61
00:13:47,750 --> 00:13:53,760
And really the two do not share their same concerns,

62
00:13:54,520 --> 00:13:59,260
they don't see the same world very much.

63
00:14:00,790 --> 00:14:03,550
And in a way the emergence of the intermediary

64
00:14:03,550 --> 00:14:10,230
intermediary as the vehicle through which capital is being formed, such as we form,

65
00:14:10,310 --> 00:14:16,250
we don't form enough, and in which savings are channeled into investment, is in many

66
00:14:16,250 --> 00:14:19,950
ways both inevitable and a great triumph.

67
00:14:20,510 --> 00:14:25,570
But it has resulted in a different political constituency.

68
00:14:26,230 --> 00:14:32,110
And one of the oldest proverbs of politics, going back all the way to Aristotle, is that

69
00:14:32,110 --> 00:14:51,970
Power follows property and power therefore is following in the sense that the employees who are the beneficiaries of these intermediaries one way or another are really in a new relationship to big business.

70
00:14:51,970 --> 00:15:18,810
On the one hand, we have the big business society and economy that is intimately related to the employees, their pension plans, their savings. And then we have a small business society which is related economically to big business, but not socially or psychologically or emotionally or even in terms of direct interest.

71
00:15:18,810 --> 00:15:28,910
And so the constituency is gone, and big business so far has not even tried to mobilize a new constituency.

72
00:15:29,950 --> 00:15:41,700
And I add to this the fact that most of you in management still see in your mind's eye the employee of 1920.

73
00:15:41,700 --> 00:15:51,780
You know, you open a management book, including mine, and read the discussion about labor relations and personnel management,

74
00:15:51,980 --> 00:16:00,860
and it is very clear that there are projections of what we learned in the early 20s in Detroit or in Pittsburgh.

75
00:16:02,210 --> 00:16:07,250
And that is not the employee of today, even in Detroit or in Pittsburgh.

76
00:16:07,250 --> 00:16:16,150
Even the dropout of today is a very different worker from the same steel

77
00:16:16,150 --> 00:16:24,740
worker of 1920. When I first knew the automobile industry, General Motors has

78
00:16:24,740 --> 00:16:31,740
just made the first educational survey of its supervisors, that was 1930, and the

79
00:16:31,740 --> 00:16:39,740
General Motors supervisor was 50 years old and therefore born in 1880

80
00:16:39,740 --> 00:16:45,590
and he had at most two half-terms of formal schooling.

81
00:16:47,360 --> 00:16:53,300
But in 1980, he went to work, you know, in 1894 as a water boy on the Burlington Railroad.

82
00:16:54,200 --> 00:17:00,140
And yet, believe me, the job was more demanding than the job is today.

83
00:17:00,800 --> 00:17:01,920
Far more he was.

84
00:17:02,240 --> 00:17:04,720
That man was supposed to run his department.

85
00:17:04,720 --> 00:17:14,300
He had no scheduler, and he had no maintenance people, and he had no personnel department, and no quality control.

86
00:17:14,620 --> 00:17:15,740
He had to run it.

87
00:17:16,280 --> 00:17:25,680
And so even at that level, the labor force has changed drastically, not only in its horizon,

88
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:33,600
and I think it is hard to underestimate the change in horizon, partly through the electronic media,

89
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:38,500
partly through the fact that so many people in this society today

90
00:17:38,500 --> 00:17:41,600
have seen the world courtesy of Uncle Sam.

91
00:17:43,360 --> 00:17:48,070
And so the constituency has changed,

92
00:17:48,570 --> 00:17:53,650
and unless big business succeeds in mobilizing the constituency

93
00:17:53,650 --> 00:17:58,470
that lies in its own employees, it will have no power base.

94
00:17:59,940 --> 00:18:04,420
And that, I think, is one of the fundamental problems,

95
00:18:04,420 --> 00:18:34,020
That the old power base, which is that local drugstore owner, if he exists at all, and largely he is sociologically simply not there, that went to small town bank, they are largely, well, yes, they are still there, but they are very clearly out of the mainstream and politically can be very effective on local issues, but not the national ones.

96
00:18:34,020 --> 00:18:59,360
But you also see no identity of interest, whereas even the medium-sized, the business with $20 or $30 million of sales and 600 employees and professional management who go to NYU and get their MBA, and there is no, they do not see themselves as belonging to the same interest.

97
00:18:59,360 --> 00:19:24,530
One of the most important phenomena in this, but also in all other developed countries, is the fragmenting of the business interest that was such a dominant force in the 19th century and spoke with one voice from Andrew Jackson, at least through Mark Hanna and even through the New Deal, and it's gone.

98
00:19:24,530 --> 00:19:51,920
And now I think you have a major problem, how to mobilize the new constituency in an employee society in which 95% or 92% of all national product goes out in the form of wages and salaries, and most of the rest is self-employed and doesn't profit either.

99
00:19:51,920 --> 00:19:57,560
and in which the only real source of capital formation,

100
00:19:57,560 --> 00:20:03,280
I do not believe that we will see a resurgence of personal saving,

101
00:20:03,460 --> 00:20:05,360
no matter what the tax laws are,

102
00:20:05,780 --> 00:20:10,980
simply because most of us feel that we are saving through the intermediaries.

103
00:20:12,120 --> 00:20:14,880
And we do, compulsory more or less.

104
00:20:15,360 --> 00:20:18,940
And when you add it all up, that savings rate is very high.

105
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:19,940
It isn't low.

106
00:20:20,600 --> 00:20:26,080
The story of the low savings rate is largely, I think, a statistical delusion.

107
00:20:26,080 --> 00:20:32,640
We have a very high savings rate, compulsory savings rate through employee benefit plans.

108
00:20:33,460 --> 00:20:37,400
And that is of the order and Social Security, which isn't saving at all.

109
00:20:38,380 --> 00:20:41,240
Economically, what is seen by the employee is saving.

110
00:20:41,240 --> 00:20:43,940
and so if you add it all up

111
00:20:43,940 --> 00:20:45,920
it's about a 35%

112
00:20:45,920 --> 00:20:48,000
rate in which

113
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,860
current earnings of the

114
00:20:50,860 --> 00:20:52,960
employee are not available to him

115
00:20:52,960 --> 00:20:55,710
for consumption and

116
00:20:55,710 --> 00:20:57,370
that they are not capital

117
00:20:57,370 --> 00:20:59,490
formation either he doesn't know

118
00:20:59,490 --> 00:21:00,330
and couldn't know

119
00:21:00,330 --> 00:21:03,510
and so I don't think you will

120
00:21:03,510 --> 00:21:05,390
see a revival of the personal

121
00:21:05,390 --> 00:21:07,310
savings rate I don't see how

122
00:21:07,310 --> 00:21:09,050
it can possibly happen

123
00:21:09,050 --> 00:21:11,130
and so how do you

124
00:21:11,130 --> 00:21:13,250
mobilize the

125
00:21:13,250 --> 00:21:23,390
existing nexus of interest between the employee and the economic system through the pension

126
00:21:23,390 --> 00:21:31,110
plan, through the indirect stock ownership, and through the interest in the job, the stake

127
00:21:31,110 --> 00:21:36,530
in the job, so that it becomes, again, a political force.

128
00:21:36,530 --> 00:21:47,020
And I think this is the major challenge to business leadership, and I haven't seen much attempts to do it.

129
00:21:47,440 --> 00:21:56,420
My prejudices show, let me say, the only attempt I see, which is the ESOP, happens to be a dangerous delusion.

130
00:21:56,420 --> 00:22:00,320
for any but the smallest business

131
00:22:00,320 --> 00:22:04,760
in which it is a good way of milking the employees

132
00:22:04,760 --> 00:22:07,840
to keep alive a dying business.

133
00:22:08,300 --> 00:22:10,120
But that's all it can do.

134
00:22:10,800 --> 00:22:14,020
We have enough experience with this particular instrument

135
00:22:14,020 --> 00:22:15,160
to know that.

136
00:22:15,740 --> 00:22:18,480
This is the wrong way of doing it.

137
00:22:18,480 --> 00:22:22,000
Even though the objective is right,

138
00:22:22,160 --> 00:22:23,680
the means are inappropriate,

139
00:22:24,280 --> 00:22:25,920
but the objective is right.

140
00:22:26,420 --> 00:22:36,020
How do you mobilize the latent potential of a parallel of interest that has to be centered around the job

141
00:22:36,020 --> 00:22:41,760
so that you can mobilize a new majority?

142
00:22:42,060 --> 00:22:46,540
The phrase is hackneyed, but the reality is there.

143
00:22:47,060 --> 00:22:48,260
That's the first point.

144
00:22:48,260 --> 00:22:57,410
And the second point is that in all developed countries, and not just in this,

145
00:22:57,410 --> 00:23:09,280
the old integrative mechanisms to create a basis for action in the political arena

146
00:23:09,280 --> 00:23:17,910
is at best running on one cylinder with five cylinders kind of sputtering.

147
00:23:18,930 --> 00:23:22,450
That's the beginning of our history as a nation.

148
00:23:22,910 --> 00:23:26,530
It's a great debate over the danger of factionalism.

149
00:23:26,530 --> 00:23:33,380
And as an answer to it, we invented the political party.

150
00:23:33,620 --> 00:23:37,240
The political party is fundamentally an American invention

151
00:23:37,240 --> 00:23:45,080
and perhaps the most lasting legacy of James Madison as a very great political philosopher,

152
00:23:45,960 --> 00:23:52,140
which was the answer to faction, in which factions were brought together,

153
00:23:52,140 --> 00:23:59,040
united behind a goal such the election of a president or a majority in the Congress

154
00:23:59,040 --> 00:24:04,060
so that they were forced to submerge factional,

155
00:24:04,060 --> 00:24:10,180
which always means single cause interests, into a program.

156
00:24:11,060 --> 00:24:18,060
And we did it non-ideologically, always a kind of...

157
00:24:18,060 --> 00:24:21,920
Our parties were a one percent tincture of ideology.

158
00:24:22,140 --> 00:24:30,140
Anti-Europeans with about a 25% ideological content.

159
00:24:30,140 --> 00:24:36,140
The European parties were always far more interest parties than they were supposed to be.

160
00:24:36,140 --> 00:24:49,830
But they used common slogans, ideological slogans, to bring about a long coalition of interests.

161
00:24:49,830 --> 00:25:14,430
And if you are interested in that part of the history of ideas, that is, the history of political parties, you will see how skillful the 19th century was. Perhaps the last ones built on the old basis were still the European social democratic parties.

162
00:25:14,430 --> 00:25:22,360
And what has happened, let me say, is that this worked too well.

163
00:25:24,290 --> 00:25:33,100
It reached the point at which essentially the original designers of parties aim,

164
00:25:33,860 --> 00:25:36,880
where you had consensus in society.

165
00:25:37,400 --> 00:25:42,230
And as a result, the party no longer does its job.

166
00:25:42,230 --> 00:25:52,450
It used to be in American politics that a landslide was a president elected by more than 60% of the vote.

167
00:25:53,270 --> 00:25:58,790
A landslide today is a president elected by 50.1% of the vote.

168
00:25:59,960 --> 00:26:04,580
Every single electoral district has become a swing district.

169
00:26:04,580 --> 00:26:21,860
When I moved up to Vermont in 1942 and went to register, a very nice lady in North Pennington said, won't you register as a Democrat? And I said, why? And she said, we need a few respectable Democrats here.

170
00:26:23,080 --> 00:26:31,710
But six years later, when I was going to be in San Francisco at the Dewey Truman election

171
00:26:31,710 --> 00:26:35,270
and went down for an absentee ballot, she said,

172
00:26:35,270 --> 00:26:44,770
I can mail one to you, you can fill one out here, or I can put it in the Republican box for you myself.

173
00:26:45,710 --> 00:26:46,690
That was Vermont.

174
00:26:47,250 --> 00:26:48,870
That is no longer true.

175
00:26:49,010 --> 00:26:51,790
Even Vermont is a swing district, even Mississippi.

176
00:26:51,790 --> 00:26:58,970
And when you have nothing but 50% districts, in which there is no majority,

177
00:27:00,570 --> 00:27:08,220
then the small splinter group, the faction, acquires veto powers.

178
00:27:09,410 --> 00:27:21,050
And all during the 19th century, the Women's Christian Temperance Union tried to use the 3 to 5 to 8% of the voted command

179
00:27:21,050 --> 00:27:30,770
to force candidates to accept temperance, accept prohibition, and it never got any place

180
00:27:30,770 --> 00:27:39,670
because very few districts were so evenly balanced that a 5% veto could swing the election.

181
00:27:40,370 --> 00:27:43,770
And in 1918, as you know, it came to pass.

182
00:27:43,770 --> 00:28:05,490
And then the same year or two later, you had in India a very small group, which Gandhi mobilized, basically brought the world's greatest power of the time to its knees, because England by that time had become evenly balanced.

183
00:28:05,490 --> 00:28:10,250
And that lesson now has become accepted and understood by everybody.

184
00:28:10,990 --> 00:28:19,510
And so very small factional groups commanding microscopic fractions,

185
00:28:19,710 --> 00:28:22,730
sprinters of the vote, have a veto power.

186
00:28:22,950 --> 00:28:26,530
They cannot produce action, but they can block action.

187
00:28:27,870 --> 00:28:30,110
Dean Dill talked about Business Day.

188
00:28:30,110 --> 00:28:37,270
I think the active support for Business Day is probably one-tenth or one percent if it's that large.

189
00:28:38,030 --> 00:28:42,250
And yet that is large enough in some areas to block.

190
00:28:44,040 --> 00:28:55,100
And so the parties have lost their power to integrate a majority and are becoming the victims of factions.

191
00:28:55,100 --> 00:29:25,020
And if you want to read what this means, the best text on it is still the Federalist Papers, where they were so conscious of that danger and of the fact that this would paralyze the political process and would make impossible any action for a program that basically thinks through priorities,

192
00:29:25,020 --> 00:29:45,790
That basically decides at what point one balances one desirable good against another, that in other words can establish political action. And that induces then the paralysis we have now.
